¶
{{smaller block|<poem>What in me is dark
Illumine, what is ''low'' raise and support.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 23.}}}}
{{smaller block|There is hardly a spirit upon earth so ''mean'' and contracted as to centre all regards on its own interests.
{{right|''Bp. Berkeley.''}}}}
{{smaller block|This vice of intemperance is the arch-abomination of our natures, tending . . . to drag down the soul to the slavery of ''grovelling'' lusts. {{right|''Everett'', Orations, I. 374.}}}}
'''II.'''† ''n.'' A person who is abjectly base, servile, or dependent; a caitiff or menial.
{{smaller block|Yea, the ''abjects'' gathered themselves together against me, and I knew it not. {{right|Ps. xxxv. 15.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>We are the queen’s ''abjects'', and must obey.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., i. 2.}}}}

'''abject'''† (ab-jekt′), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''abjectus'', pp.: see the adj.] '''1.''' To throw away; cast off or out.
{{smaller block|For that offence only Almighty God ''abjected'' Saul, that he should no more reign over Israel.
{{right|''Sir T. Elyot'', The Governour, i.}}}}
'''2.''' To make abject; humiliate; degrade.
{{smaller block|It ''abjected'' his spirit to that degree that he fell dangerously sick. {{right|''Strype'', Memorials, i. 15.}}}}
{{smaller block|What is it that can make this gallant so stoop and ''abject'' himself so basely? {{right|''Fotherby'', Atheomastix, p. 48.}}}}

'''abjectedness'''† (ab-jek′ted-nes), ''n.'' The state or condition of being abject; abjectness; humiliation.
{{smaller block|Our Saviour sunk himself to the bottom of ''abjectedness'' to exalt our condition to the contrary extreme. {{right|''Boyle.''}}}}

'''abjection''' (ab-jek′shọn), ''n.'' [< ME. ''abjeccioun'', < OF. ''abjection'', < L. ''äbjectio(n-)'', act of casting away, < ''abicere'', ''abjicere'': see ''abject'', ''a.''] '''1'''†. The

readings.c4s1:
act of casting away or down; the act of humbling or abasing; abasement.
{{smaller block|The audacite and bolde speche of Daniel signifyeth the ''abjection'' of the kynge and his realme.
{{right|''Joye'', Exp. of Daniel, ch. v.}}}}
'''2.''' The state of being cast down or away; hence, a low state; meanness of spirit; baseness; groveling humility; abjectness.
{{smaller block|That this should be termed baseness, ''abjection'' of mind, or servility, is it credible? {{right|''Hooker'', Eccl. Pol., v. § 47.}}}}
{{smaller block|Contempt for his ''abjection'' at the foul feet of the Church.
{{right|''Swinburne'', Shakespeare, p. 80.}}}}
'''3.''' Rejection; expulsion.
{{smaller block|Calvin understands by Christ’s descending into hell, that he suffered in his soul . . . all the torments of hell, even to ''abjection'' from God’s presence.
{{right|''Heylin'', Hist. of Presbyterians, p. 350.}}}}

'''abjective''' (ab-jek′tiv), ''a.'' [< ''abject'' + ''-ive.''] Tending to abase; demoralizing: as, ''abjective'' influence. ''Pall Mall Gazette.''

'''abjectly''' (ab′jekt-li), ''adv.'' In an abject, mean, or servile manner.
{{smaller block|See the statue which I create. It is ''abjectly'' servile to my will, and has no capacity whatever to gainsay it.
{{right|''H. James'', Subs. and Shad., p. 40.}}}}

'''abjectness''' (ab′jekt-nes), ''n.'' The state or quality of being abject, mean-spirited, or degraded; abasement; servility.
{{smaller block|When a wild animal is subdued to ''abjectness'', all its interest is gone. {{right|''Higginson'', Oldport Days, p. 37.}}}}

